  </body>
</html>
```

{% aside type="note" %}
**What's happening here:**

- The CesiumJS library is loaded from a <abbr title="Content Delivery Network">CDN</abbr>
- We create a full-screen `#map` container in the HTML for the 3D globe
- Three control panels are added: datasets, controls, and legend
- CSS styles make the panels semi-transparent and position them over the map
- The `type="module"` allows us to use modern JavaScript features
{% /aside %}

### 2.2: JavaScript

Now create the `script.js` file.

#### CesiumJS Concepts

First, let us go over the basic concepts of any CesiumJS app.

At the core of CesiumJS is the **Viewer**, which provides the main container for your application.
The **Viewer** contains a **Scene** (the 3D rendering context), which in turn contains the **Globe** (the 3D Earth with **Terrain** and **Imagery**) and manages the [**Camera**](https://cesium.com/learn/cesiumjs-learn/cesiumjs-camera/) for navigation and viewpoint control.

To add your own data, you work with **DataSources** and **Entities**.
**DataSources** load and manage groups of related data (like GeoJSON files), automatically converting geographic features into renderable objects.
Each feature becomes an [**Entity**](https://cesium.com/learn/cesiumjs-learn/cesiumjs-creating-entities/), which is a high-level object that can have various [**Geometries**](https://cesium.com/learn/cesiumjs-learn/cesiumjs-geometry-appearances/) like points, cylinders, polylines, or polygons.
Each **geometry** has a material.
Entities are declarative: you specify what you want (a red cylinder at this location) and the Scene renders it through the 3D graphics pipeline, positioning it correctly relative to the Globe's coordinate system.

Here's an indented tree to clarify the relations between these concepts:

```
Viewer
├── Scene
│   ├── Globe
│   │   ├── TerrainProvider
│   │   └── ImageryProviders
│   ├── Primitives
│   │   └── Cesium3DTilesVoxelProvider
│   ├── Camera
│   └── ScreenSpaceEventHandler
├── Entities
│   └── Geometries (per entity)
│       ├── Cylinder
│       │   └── Materials
│       ├── Point
│       │   └── Materials
│       ├── Polyline
│       │   └── Materials
│       ├── Polygon
│       │   └── Materials
│       └── Other 3D geometries
│           └── Materials
└── DataSources (eg GeoJsonDataSource)
    └── Entities

```

#### Step 2.3: Create the Basic Map

Let's start with the most basic 3D map. CesiumJS has a lot of features turned on by default that we don't need.
We'll turn these off first.

```js
// Step 1: Create the 3D viewer in the HTML element with id "map"
const viewer = new Cesium.Viewer("map", {
  // Turn off features we don't need for a cleaner interface
  animation: false,        // No animation controls
  timeline: false,         // No timeline at bottom
  fullscreenButton: false, // No fullscreen button
  vrButton: false,         // No VR mode button
  sceneModePicker: false,  // No 2D/3D mode picker
  baseLayerPicker: false,  // No background map picker
  navigationHelpButton: false, // No navigation help
  geocoder: false,         // No search box
  homeButton: false,       // No home button
});
```

{% aside type="note" %}
**Test it:** Save both files and start a local server in your `webmap` folder:

```bash
python3 -m http.server 8000
```

Visit `http://localhost:8000`, you should see a 3D Earth.
{% /aside %}

#### Step 2.4: Set Up Underground Visualization

Geotechnical data is underground, so we need to configure CesiumJS to show underground features:

```js
// Step 2: Configure for underground visualization
const globe = viewer.scene.globe;

// Make the ground semi-transparent so we can see underground
globe.translucency.enabled = true;
globe.translucency.frontFaceAlphaByDistance = new Cesium.NearFarScalar(
  200, // When camera is close (200m)
  0.1, // Ground is very transparent (10%)
  800, // When camera is far (800m)  
  0.7  // Ground is more opaque (70%)
);
globe.translucency.backFaceAlpha = 1.0; // Keep bottom of ground solid
globe.undergroundColor = Cesium.Color.GREY;

// Allow camera to go underground
viewer.scene.screenSpaceCameraController.enableCollisionDetection = false;

// Set camera to look at Hong Kong (where our demo data is located)
viewer.camera.setView({
  destination: Cesium.Cartesian3.fromDegrees(114.20685352, 22.23496, 1325),
  orientation: {
    heading: 0.0319,    // Rotation left-right
    pitch: -0.19935,    // Rotation up-down (looking down)
    roll: 6.28318,      // Rotation around viewing direction
  },
});
```

#### Step 2.5: Load Your Borehole Data

Let's start with the basic pattern for loading data in CesiumJS.
We'll start with the borehole data:

```js
// Basic data loading pattern
Cesium.GeoJsonDataSource.load("locations.geojson", {
  clampToGround: false, // Allow 3D positioning
})
.then((dataSource) => {
  // Add the data to the viewer so it appears on the map
  viewer.dataSources.add(dataSource);
  console.log("Loaded boreholes:", dataSource.entities.values.length);
})
.catch((error) => {
  console.error("Error loading data:", error);
  alert("Could not load locations.geojson. Make sure the file is in the same folder.");
});
```

{% aside type="note" %}
**Core Pattern:** This is the fundamental CesiumJS data loading pattern:

1. `load()` - Gets your GeoJSON file
1. `.then()` - Runs when loading succeeds
1. `viewer.dataSources.add()` - Makes data visible on map
1. `.catch()` - Handles errors if file is missing or invalid
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" %}
**Test it:** Refresh your browser. You should now see your borehole locations on the map! They might look like simple pins or markers at first, we'll improve this next.
{% /aside %}

#### Step 2.6: Styling Borehole Data

We'll make a function to customize the appearance of the borehole data when it loads.
The boreholes are vertical lines, let's show them as cylinders with white outlines.

```js
function onLoadLocations(dataSource) {
  console.log("Loaded location data", dataSource.entities.values.length);

  for (const entity of dataSource.entities.values) {
    const holeType = entity.properties.HOLE_TYPE.getValue();
    const holeId = entity.properties.HOLE_ID.getValue();

    const coordinates =
      entity.polyline && entity.polyline.positions
        ? entity.polyline.positions.getValue()
        : null;

    // Remove the default polyline rendering
    if (entity.polyline) {
      entity.polyline = undefined;
    }

    // Remove the default point rendering
    if (entity.marker) {
      entity.marker = undefined;
    }

    if (!coordinates || coordinates.length < 2) {
      console.warn(`No valid coordinates for hole ${holeId}`);
      return;
    }

    const [top, bottom] = coordinates;
    const topCartographic = Cesium.Cartographic.fromCartesian(top);
    const bottomCartographic = Cesium.Cartographic.fromCartesian(bottom);

    const lon = topCartographic.longitude * Cesium.Math.DEGREES_PER_RADIAN;
    const lat = topCartographic.latitude * Cesium.Math.DEGREES_PER_RADIAN;
    const topElevation = topCartographic.height;
    const bottomElevation = bottomCartographic.height;

    const length = Math.abs(topElevation - bottomElevation);
    const centerElevation = (topElevation + bottomElevation) / 2;

    const color = Cesium.Color.WHITE;

    dataSource.entities.add({
      position: Cesium.Cartesian3.fromDegrees(lon, lat, centerElevation),
      cylinder: new Cesium.CylinderGraphics({
        topRadius: 3,
        bottomRadius: 3,
        length: length,
        fill: false,
        outline: true,
        outlineColor: color,
        outlineWidth: 1,
        outlineOpacity: 0.5,
      }),
      properties: entity.properties,
      name: holeId,
    });
  }
}

// Basic data loading pattern
Cesium.GeoJsonDataSource.load("locations.geojson", {
  clampToGround: false, // Allow 3D positioning
})
.then((dataSource) => {
  // Add the data to the viewer so it appears on the map
  viewer.dataSources.add(dataSource);
  onLoadLocations(dataSource) // Add this to style the boreholes
  console.log("Loaded boreholes:", dataSource.entities.values.length);
})
.catch((error) => {
  console.error("Error loading data:", error);
  alert("Could not load locations.geojson. Make sure the file is in the same folder.");
});
```

#### Step 2.7: Coloring the Boreholes by Type

For effective data visualization, we need consistent color coding across different data types.
We'll use [D3.js](https://d3js.org/) scales to handle this systematically.

Scales are functions that take your actual data values as input and return appropriate visual representations (like colors) as output.
Read more about D3 scales [here](https://observablehq.com/@d3/introduction-to-d3s-scales?collection=@d3/d3-scale).

We'll use different scale types based on our data:

- **Ordinal scales** for categorical data like weathering grades (I, II, III, IV, V, VI), each grade maps to one specific color
- **Sequential scales** for continuous numeric data like SPT N-values (0-100), creates smooth color gradients across the range

For our borehole data, we have 11 different types. We want each type to be a different color.
D3 provides different [categorical color sets](https://observablehq.com/@d3/color-schemes#categorical), we'll use `schemeSet3`.

<!-- <svg viewBox="0 0 12 1" style="display:block;width:396px;height:33px;margin:0 -14px;cursor:pointer;"
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">
<rect x="0" width="1" height="1" fill="#8dd3c7" />
<rect x="1" width="1" height="1" fill="#ffffb3" />
<rect x="2" width="1" height="1" fill="#bebada" />
<rect x="3" width="1" height="1" fill="#fb8072" />
<rect x="4" width="1" height="1" fill="#80b1d3" />
<rect x="5" width="1" height="1" fill="#fdb462" />
<rect x="6" width="1" height="1" fill="#b3de69" />
<rect x="7" width="1" height="1" fill="#fccde5" />
<rect x="8" width="1" height="1" fill="#d9d9d9" />
<rect x="9" width="1" height="1" fill="#bc80bd" />
<rect x="10" width="1" height="1" fill="#ccebc5" />
<rect x="11" width="1" height="1" fill="#ffed6f" />
</svg> -->

First let's add some imports at the top of your file and the key for the hole types.
This is categorical data so we'll need an [ordinal D3 scale](https://observablehq.com/@d3/d3-scaleordinal) for coloring the different hole types.
It maps each distinct hole type to a specific color from the Set3 palette.

```js
import {
  interpolateYlGnBu,    // Yellow-Green-Blue gradient (for weathering)
  interpolateCividis,   // Colorblind-friendly gradient (for SPT values)
  schemeSet3,          // Categorical colors (for hole types)
} from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/d3-scale-chromatic@3/+esm";
import {
  scaleOrdinal,        // For discrete categories (hole types)
  scaleSequential,     // For continuous ranges (SPT N-values)
} from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/d3-scale/+esm";


// Define your borehole types and their meanings
const agsHoleTypes = {
  "CP+RO+RC": "CPT + Rotary Open + Rotary Cored",
  "CP+RC+RO": "CPT + Rotary Cored + Rotary Open",
  "CP+RO": "CPT + Rotary Open", 
  "RO+CP": "Rotary Open + CPT",
  SCP: "Standard Penetration Test",
  VC: "Vibro Core",
  RC: "Rotary Cored",
  Grab: "Grab Sample",
  RCG: "Rotary Cored + Grab",
  "IP+W+RCG": "In-situ Piezometer + Water + Rotary Cored + Grab",
  "IP+W": "In-situ Piezometer + Water",
  TP: "Trial Pit",
};

const holeTypeColorScale = scaleOrdinal()
  .domain(Object.keys(agsHoleTypes))
  .range(schemeSet3)
  .unknown("#999999");
```

Now in the `onLoadLocations` replace the `const color = Cesium.Color.WHITE;` with this:

```js
const color = Cesium.Color.fromCssColorString(holeTypeColorScale(holeType));
```

We need legends too for people to be able to decode the colors. This is a beyond the scope of this guide, so add this to the imports at the top

```js
import { createSequentialLegend, createOrdinalLegend } from "./legend.js";
```

and just copy the code below to a new file called `legend.js`.

```js
// Legend generation functions
// Adapted from https://observablehq.com/@d3/color-legend
export function createSequentialLegend({
  scale,
  title,
  width = 200,
  height = 20,
}) {
  const container = document.createElement("section");
  container.classList.add("legend-section");

  const titleEl = document.createElement("h4");
  titleEl.textContent = title;
  container.appendChild(titleEl);

  const canvas = document.createElement("canvas");
  canvas.width = width;
  canvas.height = height;
  canvas.style.display = "block";
  canvas.style.marginBottom = "4px";
  canvas.style.border = "1px solid #d9d9d9";
  const context = canvas.getContext("2d");

  const [start, end] = scale.domain();

  for (let i = 0; i < width; ++i) {
    const value = start + ((end - start) * i) / (width - 1);
    context.fillStyle = scale(value);
    context.fillRect(i, 0, 1, height);
  }

  // Add min/max labels
  const labelsDiv = document.createElement("div");
  labelsDiv.style.display = "flex";
  labelsDiv.style.justifyContent = "space-between";
  labelsDiv.style.fontSize = "10px";
  labelsDiv.innerHTML = `<span>${start}</span><span>${end}</span>`;

  container.appendChild(canvas);
  container.appendChild(labelsDiv);

  return container;
}

export function createOrdinalLegend({ scale, title, config = null }) {
  const container = document.createElement("section");
  container.classList.add("legend-section");

  const titleEl = document.createElement("h4");
  titleEl.textContent = title;
  container.appendChild(titleEl);

  const itemsDiv = document.createElement("div");
  scale.domain().forEach((value) => {
    const item = document.createElement("div");
    item.className = "legend-item";

    // Use full name from config if available, otherwise use the value
    const displayName = config?.[value] ? config[value] : value;

    item.innerHTML = `
      <div class="legend-circle" style="background-color: ${scale(
        value
      )}"></div>
      <span>${displayName}</span>
    `;
    itemsDiv.appendChild(item);
  });

  container.appendChild(itemsDiv);
  return container;
}
```

#### Step 2.8: Add Weathering Data

Now let's add the weathering dataset with LineString styling. Weathering data shows rock deterioration along borehole depths.
Weathering data follows a classification scheme with grades I (fresh rock) through VI (residual soil) so we need an ordinal scale that maps the grades to colors.\
We'll interpolat the `YlGnBu` color scheme.

```js
const weatheringGrades = [
  { grade: "I", value: 0 },
  { grade: "II", value: 1 / 5 },
  { grade: "II/III", value: 1.5 / 5 },
  { grade: "III/II", value: 1.5 / 5 },
  { grade: "III", value: 2 / 5 },
  { grade: "III/IV", value: 2.5 / 5 },
  { grade: "IV/III", value: 2.5 / 5 },
  { grade: "IV", value: 3 / 5 },
  { grade: "IV/V", value: 3.5 / 5 },
  { grade: "V/IV", value: 3.5 / 5 },
  { grade: "V", value: 4 / 5 },
  { grade: "VI", value: 5 / 5 },
].map((d) => ({ ...d, color: interpolateYlGnBu(d.value) })); // Make a color scheme from the 0 - 5 scale


const weatheringGradeColorScale = scaleOrdinal()
  .domain(weatheringGrades.map((d) => d.grade))
  .range(weatheringGrades.map((d) => d.color));

function onLoadWeatheringData(dataSource) {
  console.log("Loaded weathering data:", dataSource.entities.values.length);

  for (const entity of dataSource.entities.values) {
    const wetheringGrade = entity.properties?.WETH_GRAD?.getValue();
    const color = weatheringGradeColorScale(wetheringGrade);

    if (entity.billboard) {
      entity.billboard = undefined;
      // Add a point for point geometries
      entity.point = new Cesium.PointGraphics({
        pixelSize: 4,
        color: Cesium.Color.fromCssColorString(color),
        outlineColor: Cesium.Color.WHITE,
        outlineWidth: 1,
      });
    }

    if (entity.polyline) {
      entity.polyline.material = Cesium.Color.fromCssColorString(color);
      entity.polyline.width = 7;
      entity.polyline.clampToGround = false;
    }
  }
}


Cesium.GeoJsonDataSource.load("weathering.geojson", {
    clampToGround: false, // Allow 3D positioning
  })
  .then((dataSource) => {
    viewer.dataSources.add(dataSource);
    onLoadWeatheringData(dataSource)
    console.log("Loaded weathering data:", dataSource.entities.values.length);
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error("Error loading data:", error);
    alert("Could not load weathering.geojson. Make sure the file is in the same folder.");
  });

```

#### Step 2.9: Add SPT Data

And finally, the SPT data. For this we need to map the continuous numeric data SPT N-values (0-100) to a color across the range using a [**Sequential scale**](https://observablehq.com/@d3/sequential-scales).
The SPT's are point geometries.

```js
// Creates smooth color gradient from 0 to 100 using colorblind-friendly Cividis color range
const sptScale = scaleSequential(interpolateCividis)
  .domain([0, 100]);

function onLoadSptData(dataSource) {
  console.log("Loaded ispt data:", dataSource.entities.values.length);

  for (const entity of dataSource.entities.values) {
    const sptNValue = entity.properties?.ISPT_NVAL?.getValue();
    const color = sptScale(sptNValue);
    // remove default pin for points
    if (entity.billboard) {
      entity.billboard = undefined;
    }
    if (!sptNValue) {
      continue;
    }

    entity.point = new Cesium.PointGraphics({
      pixelSize: 4,
      color: Cesium.Color.fromCssColorString(color),
      outlineColor: Cesium.Color.WHITE,
      outlineWidth: 1,
      heightReference: Cesium.HeightReference.NONE,
    });
  }
}

Cesium.GeoJsonDataSource.load("ispt.geojson", {
    clampToGround: false, // Allow 3D positioning
  })
  .then((dataSource) => {
    viewer.dataSources.add(dataSource);
    onLoadSptData(dataSource);
    console.log("Loaded ispt data:", dataSource.entities.values.length);
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error("Error loading data:", error);
    alert("Could not load ispt.geojson. Make sure the file is in the same folder.");
  });

```

#### Step 2.10: Structured Data Management

If you load the map now you'll see all three datasets at the same time which is not clear nor useful.

We'll restructure the code a bit to avoid repetition and prepare for toggling dataset visibility.

First let's organize our data in an array to handle multiple datasets.

```js
// Step 4a: Define our dataset structure
const datasets = [
  {
    id: "locations",
    label: "Borehole Locations",
    enabled: false,
    dataSource: null,
    legendElement: createOrdinalLegend({
      scale: holeTypeColorScale,
      title: "Hole Types",
      config: agsHoleTypes,
    }),
    onLoad: onLoadLocations,
  },
  {
    id: "weathering",
    label: "Weathering",
    enabled: false,
    dataSource: null,
    legendElement: createOrdinalLegend({
      scale: weatheringGradeColorScale,
      title: "Weathering Grade",
      config: null,
    }),
    onLoad: onLoadWeatheringData,
  },
  {
    id: "ispt",
    label: "Standard Penetration Test",
    enabled: true,
    dataSource: null,
    legendElement: createSequentialLegend({
      scale: sptScale,
      title: "SPT N Value",
    }),
    onLoad: onLoadSptData,
  },
];
```

#### Step 2.11: Loading Function That Uses Our Structure

This part is the same for each dataset so we'll turn it into a function.

```js
// Step 4b: Generic loading function
function loadDataset(dataset) {
  return Cesium.GeoJsonDataSource.load(`${dataset.id}.geojson`, {
    clampToGround: false,
  })
  .then((dataSource) => {
    dataset.dataSource = dataSource;      // Store reference for later use
    dataset.onLoad(dataSource);           // Apply custom styling
    dataSource.show = dataset.enabled;    // Show/hide based on enabled state
    viewer.dataSources.add(dataSource);   // Add to the map
    return dataSource;
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error(`Error loading ${dataset.id}.geojson:`, error);
  });
}

// Step 4c: Load all datasets
Promise.allSettled(datasets.map(dataset => loadDataset(dataset)))
  .then((results) => {
    console.log("Finished loading all datasets");
    results.forEach((result, index) => {
      if (result.status === "rejected") {
        console.warn(`Failed to load ${datasets[index].id}:`, result.reason);
      }
    });
  });
```

{% aside type="note" %}
**What's happening:**

1. `Promise.allSettled()` loads all datasets in parallel, which is faster than one-by-one
1. Error handling per dataset, one failure doesn't break everything
1. The structure makes it easy to add a new dataset. Just an an object to the array.
{% /aside %}

#### Step 2.12: User Interface Interactivity

Now we'll add interactive controls so users can toggle datasets on/off and see legends explaining the color coding. The controls will automatically generate checkboxes for each dataset and show/hide the corresponding legends.

```js
function updateLegendDisplay() {
  for (const dataset of datasets) {
    if (dataset.legendElement) {
      dataset.legendElement.style.display = dataset.enabled ? "block" : "none";
    }
  }
}

function generateDatasetControls() {
  const controlsSection = document.getElementById("datasets");

  const controlsHTML = datasets
    .map(
      (dataset) => `
  <div class="checkbox-item">
    <input type="checkbox" id="${dataset.id}-toggle" ${
        dataset.enabled ? "checked" : ""
      }>
    <label for="${dataset.id}-toggle">${dataset.label}</label>
  </div>
`
    )
    .join("");

  controlsSection.innerHTML = controlsSection.innerHTML + controlsHTML;

  // Add event listeners to checkboxes
  for (const dataset of datasets) {
    const checkbox = document.getElementById(`${dataset.id}-toggle`);
    checkbox.addEventListener("change", (event) => {
      dataset.enabled = event.target.checked;
      if (dataset.dataSource) {
        dataset.dataSource.show = dataset.enabled;
      }
      updateLegendDisplay();
    });
  }
}

generateDatasetControls();

// Add legend elements to DOM and set initial visibility
const legendEl = document.querySelector("#legend");
for (const dataset of datasets) {
  dataset.legendElement.style.display = dataset.enabled ? "block" : "none";
  legendEl.appendChild(dataset.legendElement);
}

// Load all datasets
Promise.allSettled(datasets.map((dataset) => loadDataset(dataset))).then(
  (results) => {
    for (const result of results) {
      if (result.status === "rejected") {
        console.warn("Failed to load dataset:", result.reason);
      }
    }
  }
);

// Globe opacity slider
document.querySelector("#alpha").addEventListener("input", (event) => {
  const alpha = event.target.valueAsNumber;

  // Update translucency using distance-based approach
  globe.translucency.frontFaceAlphaByDistance.nearValue = alpha;
  globe.translucency.frontFaceAlphaByDistance.farValue = alpha;
  // imageryLayer.alpha = alpha;
});

// 3D buildings toggle
document
  .querySelector("#buildings-toggle")
  .addEventListener("change", (event) => {
    osmBuildings.show = event.target.checked;
  });
```

With these controls in place, users will see:
- **Dataset toggles** in the top-left panel to show/hide each data type
- **Color legends** in the right panel that appear/disappear with their corresponding datasets
- **Basemap opacity slider** to adjust ground transparency for better underground visibility
- **3D buildings toggle** to show/hide OpenStreetMap building data

{% aside type="note" %}
**Final Test:** Open your map and try toggling different datasets on and off. You should see the legends update automatically and the 3D visualization respond to your controls. The combination of all three datasets provides a comprehensive view of the geotechnical investigation.
{% /aside %}

